@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title Paired-Comparison Rating — Bradley-Terry to Glicko
' Paper: Parameter Estimation in Large Dynamic Paired Comparison Experiments (Glickman, 1999)
' URL:   https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9876.00159
'        author copy (publisher page is bot-gated): https://www.glicko.net/research/glicko.pdf
' Paper: Rank Analysis of Incomplete Block Designs: I. The Method of Paired Comparisons
'        (Bradley & Terry, 1952 — the underlying model; page range unverified, so pages omitted)
' URL:   https://doi.org/10.2307/2334029
' Level: 1

component "Match outcomes\nwin / loss / draw pairs" as OUT <<app>>
component "Bradley-Terry model\nP(A beats B) from strengths" as BT <<app>>
component "Elo update\nmove by prediction error" as ELO <<async>>
component "Glicko\nrating + rating deviation RD" as GL <<host>>
database  "Strength estimate\nwith a confidence interval" as EST <<db>>
component "Decision gate\nis B stronger than A?" as GATE <<error>>

OUT ARROW_MAIN BT : fit latent strengths
BT ARROW_MAIN ELO : approximate online update\non the same likelihood
BT ARROW_MAIN GL : add per-player uncertainty
ELO ARROW_OPTIONAL EST
GL ARROW_MAIN EST
EST ARROW_MAIN GATE : compare intervals,\nnot point estimates
GATE ARROW_ERROR OUT : errorLabel(inconclusive - play more games)

note bottom of GL
  **Bradley-Terry (1952)** is the underlying model: a
  latent strength per player, with the probability of
  each pairwise outcome a logistic function of the
  difference. Elo is an //approximate online// update on
  that likelihood — a stochastic-gradient step, not a
  special case of the model (and Elo's original
  formulation used a normal, not logistic,
  distribution). Cheap, but it reports a number with
  no stated precision.
  ----
  **Ties.** Plain Bradley-Terry has no tie term at
  all. Scoring a draw as half a win is the Elo/Glicko
  convention, not a property of the model; modelling
  ties inside the paired-comparison framework needs an
  extension — Rao-Kupper (a tie threshold) or Davidson
  (an explicit tie parameter). This matters wherever a
  turn cap makes draws common.
  ----
  **Glicko's addition is the error bar.** It tracks a
  //rating deviation// alongside the rating, so a
  provisional player moves fast and a well-measured
  one moves slowly, and two ratings can be compared
  honestly. That is exactly what a regression gate
  needs: "B beats A" is only a claim once the
  intervals separate.
end note
@enduml
